{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792705986,"nonce":"Pi9aIz6sv1ru/6kbWlgFmODJAd0=","title":"LICM hoists float-to-integer conversions ahead of the test that guards them","message":"licm.rs lists Cast among the pure, non-trapping instructions it may hoist, but a float-to-integer conversion (FpToSi, FpToUi) traps in Cranelift code when the value is out of range (the HIR interpreter saturates, which hides it). Hoisting one out of a loop runs it before the range test that guarded it, so a loop-invariant operand such as 1e19, infinity or NaN traps and the program hangs.\n\nFound while adding the shared float-remainder fast path: a Python loop taking v % d with v from a list hung once the result was printed, while TypeScript tests did not hit it because their operands changed on every iteration. Any frontend that guards a conversion with a range test is exposed.\n\nFix: FpToSi and FpToUi are no longer hoisted (the same reason integer Div and Rem are excluded). Unit test: licm::tests::a_float_to_integer_conversion_is_not_speculated_but_other_casts_are. The inconsistency underneath remains open: the interpreter saturates, Cranelift traps and LLVM treats it as poison, so the tiers disagree on out-of-range conversions.","files":null}]}